  <prompt>
    `ComposeDocxProjectionBuilder.cs` is 3,085 lines and frozen by the ratchet. Bring it below 2,000 and DELETE
    its waiver entry.

    Natural seams: block projection, run projection (`ProjectRun` ~:2644), atom identification, table
    projection, numbering, and part traversal.

    The property that matters more than the line count: **projection output must be byte-for-byte
    equivalent** before and after. The projection is the only coordinate system in this architecture —
    anchors, the merge's unchanged-detection, and every gate measurement all resolve through it. A one-block
    difference in output is not a refactor, it is a silent re-architecture.

    Prove equivalence empirically over the corpus, not by argument.

    ADR-010: internal collaborators, not DI registrations. Behavior-preserving only — defects found go to
    their owning task as findings.
  </prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE backend developer. Expert in behavior-preserving refactoring and the Compose projection model. Follow ADR-010, ADR-049 and ADR-038.</role>

  <goal>
    `ComposeDocxProjectionBuilder.cs` is under 2,000 lines, its waiver is deleted, ArchTests are green, and
    projection output over the whole corpus is provably unchanged.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      `ProjectRun` (~:2644) currently keeps only three booleans from `rPr` — that narrowness is a Track A
      concern (tasks 040/041), not a Track D one. This task must NOT widen it; changing what the projection
      captures while restructuring makes the equivalence proof impossible.
    </background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="primary-edit">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Compose/ComposeDocxProjectionBuilder.cs</file>
      <file role="primary-edit">tests/Spaarke.ArchTests/GodClassGuardTests.cs</file>
